# Script function and purpose: OpenPAM policy for the hikari screen-unlock
# helper on FreeBSD. Resuming a locked session, not starting a new one.

auth include system

# Action purpose: The account group is declared explicitly, not left out, now
# that hikari-unlocker calls pam_acct_mgmt(). OpenPAM and Linux-PAM both fall
# back to the "other" policy PER FACILITY when a service declares no modules
# for one, so omitting this line does not mean "skip the account check" -- it
# means inherit whatever "other" says.
#
# On FreeBSD that inheritance is the problem. Its base "other" account group
# is, verbatim from lib/libpam/pam.d/other in the FreeBSD source tree
# (identical in releng/14.3 and main):
#
#     account         required        pam_nologin.so
#     account         required        pam_login_access.so
#     account         required        pam_unix.so
#
# which is pam_nologin -- exactly the module the `system` versus `login` choice
# below exists to avoid. Falling through to "other" would therefore reintroduce
# it by the back door and let a /var/run/nologin written during shutdown refuse
# the unlock.
#
# A deny-by-default "other", which some Linux distributions do ship, is the
# harsher case: there pam_acct_mgmt against a service with an auth group and no
# account group returns PAM_AUTH_ERR with no explanation from any module, so
# the user's correct password is refused with a bare "Authentication failure"
# and retrying never helps. Measured in the Linux container. FreeBSD is not
# that case, but the line is required for both.
#
# `include` rather than a module list, and `system` rather than `login`, both
# to mirror the auth line above: OpenPAM's include imports only the matching
# facility, so this takes the system account policy and nothing else. `login`
# is rejected here for the reason recorded in D-19 -- it carries pam_nologin,
# which governs STARTING a session, while unlocking RESUMES one, so a
# /var/run/nologin written during shutdown would lock the user out of the
# session they are trying to get back into.
account include system
